digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This work develops and evaluates HoloVAM, a tomographic volumetric additive-manufacturing approach using a Digital Micromirror Device to deliver phase-encoded holographic projections, computed with methods such as the Gerchberg-Saxton algorithm and implemented through Lee holograms, so that phase modulation and 3D beam shaping improve light efficiency, reduce support-structure constraints, and enable rapid high-resolution printing of millimeter-scale objects including cell-laden hydrogels.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When a volumetric light-based printing method has optical efficiency and resolution limitations, a projection engine that improves light use, enables phase-based beam shaping, and is demonstrated on relevant resins and hydrogels supports a new tomographic VAM research proposal."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Because tomographic amplitude patterns waste light in mostly dark pixels while phase modulation lets modulator pixels contribute by interference across the image plane, phase modulation is a plausible route to improve projection light efficiency."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Phase encoding can improve tomographic projection light efficiency relative to amplitude encoding, which sends less than 1% of incident light to the vial."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Using Lee holograms to make a DMD act as a fast phase modulator supports phase-encoded PSF modification, such as increasing depth of focus or creating multi-plane projections."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Since phase-encoded PSF modification can increase depth of focus, it can address blurring that arises when the vial diameter exceeds the depth of focus and thereby support 3D digital control of the light beam."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) The reported sub-60-second printing with a single 40 mW laser diode and reliable 31.35 μm features is consistent with the light-efficiency advantage expected from phase encoding, without proving that efficiency is the sole cause of the short print time."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Because tomographic VAM computes projection patterns and the Gerchberg-Saxton algorithm computes phase patterns that produce desired intensity distributions, holographic phase-computation methods are plausible candidates for adapting projection generation to TVAM."];
  "bg3" [label="(10,0,0) Induction-case: Implementations of tomographic VAM rely on Radon transform to compute light amplitude patterns"];
  "demo1" [label="(40,0,0) Induction-case: We demonstrate printing time of less than 60 seconds with single 40mW laser diode and reach 31.35μm features reliably"];
  "domain3" [label="(21,0,0) Deduction-rule: If space-bandwidth product of light source is optimized, then depth of focus is determined and controlled"];
  "domain4" [label="(36,0,0) Induction-common: Multiple iterative algorithms or machine learning approaches can generate two-dimensional input phase patterns to produce user-specified intensity distributions"];
  "inter1" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-conclusion: Given that tomographic patterns have mostly dark pixels, and amplitude encoding wastes light from dark pixels, therefore amplitude encoding is highly inefficient"];
  "inter7" [label="(43,0,0) Deduction-case: Currently Lee Hologram method allows use of DMD as fast phase modulator"];
  "inter8" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-conclusion: Given that vial diameter larger than depth of focus causes blurring, and current systems have limited depth of focus, therefore resolution is compromised"];
  "inter9" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-conclusion: Given that depth of focus can be increased reduces resolution limitations, and phase encoding with Axicon phase increases depth of focus, therefore phase encoding addresses resolution limitations"];
  "know1" [label="(35,0,0) Deduction-rule: If a projection system uses phase modulation, then all pixels on the modulator contribute by interference to all pixels on the image plane"];
  "know4" [label="(32,0,0) Deduction-rule: If phase encoding is used, then PSF modification can be encoded to increase depth of focus or create multi-plane projections"];
  "prob1" [label="(31,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: Amplitude encoding results in less than 1% of incident light reaching the vial"];
  "prob2" [label="(30,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: In tomographic projection patterns, most pixels are dark due to high spatial frequency information"];
  "prob3" [label="(20,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: If vial diameter is much larger than depth of focus, blurring artifacts appear and compromise resolution"];
  "prob2" -> "inter1" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "prob1" -> "inter1" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
  "prob3" -> "inter8" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "domain3" -> "inter8" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
  "know4" -> "inter9" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "inter8" -> "inter9" [label="deduction-case"];
  "inter1" -> "R2" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "know1" -> "R2" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
  "prob1" -> "R3" [label="induction-case"];
  "R2" -> "R3" [label="induction-common"];
  "know4" -> "R4" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "inter7" -> "R4" [label="deduction-case"];
  "R4" -> "R5" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "prob3" -> "R5" [label="deduction-case"];
  "demo1" -> "R8" [label="induction-case"];
  "R3" -> "R8" [label="induction-common"];
  "bg3" -> "R9" [label="induction-case"];
  "domain4" -> "R9" [label="induction-common"];
  "R5" -> "NROOT" [label="induction-case"];
  "R8" -> "NROOT" [label="induction-case"];
  "R9" -> "NROOT" [label="induction-case"];
  "inter9" -> "NROOT" [label="induction-case"];
